Place is an incredibly powerful way that we find and connect with other people, build shared memories, have a sense of purpose. And there's lots of symbols associated with place that signal that were special. So I live in New York City, and I've lived here for last 16 years. And I've developed an identity as a New Yorker, even though I didn't move here until I was already 30 years old. And in New York, there's a very strong identity. I've actually heard that even having the phone number 212 at the start of your phone number is something that New Yorkers value. and really don't want to lose once they get it because it's a powerful signal that you're a true New Yorker.

114
01:08:52.000 --> 01:09:21.000
But I will say this. I was just actually last night planning my high school reunion with my friends because I grew up in a small town called Fox Creek, 2,000 people in rural, northern Alberta and Canada. And we still talk, you know, it's now 30 years since our graduation. And we have a shared sense of identity from that place, experiences, memories, and connections that will still draw us together that will pull people together from multiple countries to get together to revisit

115
01:09:21.000 --> 01:10:04.000
those and remember them and share them. So place is incredibly important to people, whether it's a country, a state, a town or a city, or even your local community. Some communities have a really strong identity where they have like group barbecues and activities that are just community focused. So this can happen at all kinds of place. So Christina, as we heard, is from Houston. But besides identifying with Houston, she took care to mention that she does not identify with another city. Here she is again. And a big part of that identity is not only love for our city and our sports teams, but along with that comes a dislike for the city of Dallas, our rival in many things,

