118
01:11:27.000 --> 01:11:59.000
And then it can lead to negative things. If you really start to spill over this animosity into hatred, it can lead to violence, discrimination, and conflict. And so we have to be very mindful that it can be very useful to have healthy competition and healthy conflict, like the example of Houston versus Dallas and sports teams. But we have to be very mindful that there has to be a boundary where it doesn't spill over into those negative aspects of group identity. I'm wondering why many animosities around group identity are connected with people who are

119
01:11:59.000 --> 01:12:35.000
quite similar to one another. So for 90% of the people on the planet, Dallas and Houston are both cities in Texas. They have so much in common. Why do you think it is that people from Houston and Dallas focus on what makes them dissimilar. Yeah, the classic term for this is called the narcissism of small differences that we actually often become obsessed with people who are very similar to us, in part because we're comparing ourselves to them. And so this can be where you're competitive with a good friend or an office mate because they seem the most relevant comparison to you. Now, that's different from

120
01:12:35.000 --> 01:13:21.000
the way that most people think about conflict, where they think that we have conflict with the people who are most different from us, the people who have radically different belief systems or religions or come from different countries, different skin colors, different genders. But in most situations, it's actually the people who are similar to us because we see them as a relevant form of competition. I'm wondering if the same thing is true in politics. Republicans and Democrats in the United States feel like there are, you know, chasms separating them. But of course, so most people on the planet, that Republicans and Democrats within the U.S. are all Americans, and the distinction between them is probably small. Do you think this affects the way political polarization operates, Jay?

121
01:13:21.000 --> 01:13:57.000
It is definitely part of the puzzle of polarization. I would say there's two pieces of the puzzle of polarization. One is that people do have genuine disagreements about some policies like health care or immigration. But on top of that, there is what we call affect of polarization, where it's just you really don't like a group. simply because they're part of another party, even if they agree with you on the actual policies. And so this leads to all kinds of real discrimination. And I will say part of it is exaggerated. If you actually measure what people believe, the average Democrat and average Republican

122
01:13:57.000 --> 01:14:16.000
aren't that far apart. But if you ask them what they think the other party believes, they imagine that people from the other party hate them way more than they do. They imagine that people in the other party are very different from them in terms of beliefs in terms of demographics. And so it creates a funny situation where they tend to avoid each other. They now move to different places

123
01:14:16.000 --> 01:14:31.000
and communities and states. But if you actually put people from these different political parties in a conversation, they predict it will go badly and they're shocked when it goes as good as it does because their stereotypes are so negative

124
01:14:31.000 --> 01:14:46.000
and so distorted from reality that most people they're talking to once they're in the flesh and blood are actually much more personable and interesting and reasonable than they imagined. Yeah. One thing that I think many of us notice when it comes to group identity

125
01:14:46.000 --> 01:15:06.000
is that it can create very different conversations depending on who you're talking to. So, for example, you hear New Yorkers complain all the time about New York City. They'll say things like it's really dirty, there's trash everywhere, it's crowded, the housing is so expensive. But if you come in as an outsider and criticize New Yorkers,

126
01:15:06.000 --> 01:15:42.000
will fight you. Yeah. There is something about the New York identity that is about complaining, about the smell, about the garbage, about all kinds of things. But it is also a source of pride. And there's actually a funny aspect of identity in that way. And again, this goes back to this optimal distinctiveness. New York feels different than every other city in America. And the things that make it distinctive are the things that can be really frustrating as you live here day to day. But they're also the things that make it special. And when someone from the outside, criticizes those things, you defend it.

127
01:15:42.000 --> 01:16:09.000
My favorite quote on this, I saw someone post this on social media. They said everybody complains that New York smells like urine. But they said the way you have to think about it is urine happens to smell like the greatest city on earth. You're thinking about it the wrong way. We heard from a listener named Karen. She has two daughters. And when they were younger, they played in a soccer league.

128
01:16:09.000 --> 01:16:56.000
And she writes, the teams were chosen randomly, and every year you would sit with different people on different sides of the field based on what team your daughter was assigned to. So you could no longer sit with your friend whose daughter was on your daughter's team last year because they were now the enemy. It's interesting how one year you're cheering on a child because they are on your child's team and the next year you want them to lose. So it almost sounds, Jay, that, you know, Karen was taking part in a social science experiment, the way these soccer teams were constructed. but talk for a moment about how malleable group loyalties can be. So Karen's experience maps exactly onto my experience. When I was a kid, we'd play games of pick up hockey or pick up basketball or pick up football. And I had my one friend, Jaron, he and I were almost best friends.

129
01:16:56.000 --> 01:17:32.000
And if we flipped a coin and ended up on the same team, we got along famously. But if we were on the opposite team, by the end of the game, we'd be yelling and fighting with one another because we were both very competitive. And that inspired me actually to study group identity. And most of the research I started doing on this when I was a PhD student was bringing people into lab and flipping a coin and putting them on two teams. And what we found over and over and over again is just that simple random flip of a coin makes people identify with their own group and discriminate against the other group. They will give more resources to members of their own group.

130
01:17:32.000 --> 01:18:06.000
They see them as friends. They automatically trust them more and believe. what they say. And these are the things that happen, even if they don't have a chance to interact or even meet anybody in their own group, just knowing that they're part of the group is sufficient. And so this is actually called minimal groups because these effects have been found for 50 years over and over again. I actually think this is one of the most important findings in all of psychology that a flip of a coin changes our identities and radically changes how we see ourselves and how we treat other people in really deep, profound ways. And so,

131
01:18:06.000 --> 01:18:51.000
This is essentially what Karen's experiencing with her daughter and the soccer teams. This is a powerful psychological force. And it's something that it's easy to take for granted until you're in it. And you see what it does to you. But this is something that's important part of human social psychology. When we come back, what happens when you disagree with your group? You're listening to Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam.

132
01:18:51.000 --> 01:19:51.000
Psychologist J. Van Bavel studies how group identities can bridge religious, racial, and political divides. With Dominic Packer, he's the author of The Power of Us, harnessing our shared identities to improve performance, increase cooperation, and promote social harmony. As we've discussed, Jay, our group identities shape how we as individuals view the world, but being part of a group of people can be complicated. Maybe a political leader of your party or the coach of your sports team takes your group. group in a direction you might disagree with. Here is listener Miriam. I am a Jewish person. I identify very much with my group as a Jewish person, but I do not at all agree with what's happening in Israel right now in the conflict with Gaza. As a result, it makes me feel almost ashamed of my religion, and when it comes to the holidays, I'm very conflicted about

133
01:19:51.000 --> 01:20:48.000
celebrating them. So I was just wondering, my question is, how does it affect people when the group identity that they've always had suddenly manifest something that they don't agree with or they feel uncomfortable with and they feel conflicted about it? What do you think, Jay? I mean, that's a really powerful question, Shanker. So what Miriam's talking about is the fact that you can feel pride when your group does well. Again, a national group winning a gold medal, your favorite sports team. But it can also be a source of shame. Or in this case, it sounds like she's feeling a lot of cognitive dissonance. Like she feels really identified at this group and very upset about where it's going. So when I hear stories like this, I think that one of the most important things about any group,

134
01:20:48.000 --> 01:21:22.000
is to create healthy norms. And in this case, the norm that I think is really important in any healthy group is about dissent. The research on this, done by my co-author Dominic when we were in grad school, he wanted to understand why people speak up when they think their group is going wrong. And what he found was actually really counterintuitive. Many people have this notion that dissent happens when people don't like the group and want to leave it. But what actually happens is the opposite. it is often driven by people who really identify with the group and care about it and worry about

135
01:21:22.000 --> 01:21:59.000
where it's going and think that the group is going to be damaged if it continues down this direction. And so they speak up despite the fact that they know they're going to get pushback or social ostracism or punishment. Because I always just remind people, think of the terms we have for dissenters. You know, throughout history and how they're treated like heretics, how are they treated, traders, even devil's advocates. You go to a company and they'll say so-and-so is the devil's advocate. And I say, is there a meaner word you could come up for someone than devil's advocate? So like pause and think about what you're saying. And so what any group needs to do,

136
01:21:59.000 --> 01:22:42.000
whether this is a national group, part of a community, I often see this in organizations, to avoid that kind of cult mentality is to make sure that you make it safe for people to speak up in dissent, and part of it is understanding that the people who are doing it are doing it because they want to help the group and they're worried about it. And they think it's gone wrong. And so that is a fundamental norm that needs to be reinforced and ideally by leaders, because leaders can make it very hard to dissent if they punish dissenters, or they can make it easy by expressing how much they value them and how important it is. And in society, this is often through protest. This is like one of the most important signals of a healthy democracy is that

137
01:22:42.000 --> 01:23:15.000
people can peacefully protest. So this is like a signal that we're healthy in our society if we can do this, that we can speak up. I've been to countries around the world where I talk openly of my criticisms of my home country and people's eyes get wide because they would never feel safe doing that in their own country. And so this is something that is really critical in any group that you're part of is to understand the dissenters are often the people who care the most. And they're speaking up because they're concerned and giving them a platform. and hearing them out, even if you don't agree with them,

138
01:23:15.000 --> 01:23:48.000
at least taking them seriously and understanding they're doing it out of a position of care. You've done a lot of work looking at group identities in political context. Listener, Jacqueline, sent us a message about that. We definitely have a whole lot of tribalism going on in political parties, in families and friend groups, but certain groups are coded as bad or harmful. and I feel like we've collectively, we've just lost our collective culture.

139
01:23:48.000 --> 01:24:26.000
What we're taught to do is stand on the other side of the stream and point a finger at somebody and hate them because of all of the ways that they're different from us. And what I've begun doing is seeking out people that don't agree with me and doing so from a place of curiosity, trying to understand them, and as a secondary to be understood, to listen more than I talk. So we talked earlier about how outgroup hate can be as or even more motivating than in-group love. And it's possible that maybe every generation feels that we are more divided than previous

140
01:24:26.000 --> 01:24:59.000
generations. But what do you make of Jacqueline's point, Jay, about conflict between different political groups and also her approach to addressing it? So Jacqueline puts her finger on a feeling that many people right now in the United States have and many other countries. In the United States, as you said, this is a political divide in many cases. But hate can come from all kinds of angles. It can be driven by racial hatred. It can be driven by religious hatred. So I think we have to take it always very seriously that it is a dangerous thing because it is very powerful and motivating. And the other

141
01:24:59.000 --> 01:25:43.000
thing to understand is one of the reasons why people are so passionately hate other groups is because it's driven from a position of morality. I actually have studied this in have several papers on it, that when people hate something, they say it's because that group or that belief system violates their sense of morality. And the moment that something violates your sense of morality, you see it through the lens of black and white, good and evil, right and wrong. And it's hard for you to listen. If you see things that way, it's hard for you to treat other people like human beings when you think that they're morally evil. But what Jacqueline's doing is almost textbook what social psychologists have found is actually really important to get past

142
01:25:43.000 --> 01:26:27.000
these issues, which is that you need to realize that you can reach out to people and they're not the evil, sinister beings that you believe. Some of them might be, but often our mental images and stereotypes are way worse than reality for the entire group, and we shouldn't apply them to everybody in the group. The other thing that you need to understand is that if you approach it from a position of curiosity and you listen and reach out to other people to hear their perspectives, that turns out to be the single thing that is most powerful at putting other people at ease. Because in these types of intergroup conflicts, people, when they're interacting with someone from the other group, expect to be criticized or manipulated. And so they immediately move to the defensive and shut down.

143
01:26:27.000 --> 01:27:10.000
But if you approach it from a perspective of curiosity and listening, it turns out you actually are more persuasive. They actually appreciate. the conversation more, you actually start to shatter their stereotypes as well as your own, and that becomes the foundation for having a real relationship that's built on mutual empathy and respect. And so I can't stress enough how the world would be a better place if we had many more people like Jacqueline. You know, Jacqueline's comment made me think about conversations we've had about intellectual humility. You and some colleagues recently published an interesting paper on the role of collective intellectual humility and how it might be easier as a group than

144
01:27:10.000 --> 01:27:52.000
as an individual to be humble about what we know or don't know. Tell me more about this work, Jay. Yeah, a topic that really has fascinated me for the last half decade or so is intellectual humility. And what this means is essentially openness to different people, to different experiences, without getting threatened or defensive if you disagree or they challenge you. And this is often considered a virtue. It's an intellectual virtue that any of us can cultivate. Now, most of the research on this has been at the individual level, that some individuals are more intellectually humble. And it turns out people who are are less polarized, more willing to listen to people who are different than them, more willing to accept criticism of their own perspectives and accept kind of

145
01:27:52.000 --> 01:28:23.000
well-rounded views of things. So it turns out to be a real superpower. But it also operates at the group level. So you can be part of a group and have an openness. to dissent and criticism in the group. And sometimes even in a group, it can be useful to have someone who's low on intellectual humility because they push everybody to think about something that they're not taking seriously. And they continue to pound away at it. This is often the psychology of a whistleblower that they're not going to drop it because they think something really bad is going on.

146
01:28:23.000 --> 01:28:59.000
And so they're going to continue to hammer at home or get the word out to the public about it because they're very deeply concerned about it. So again, this is partly the role of leaders. to create norms. And when I run my research meetings every Friday, we have a research team of 15 to 20 people who meet, if I'm talking about a paper we're working on or something that I've written and I share it for feedback, I share my opinion last because what I learned is once I became a professor, the students saw me as powerful. And if I would say I really like something or disliked it, they would go around the room trying to find reasons to agree with me. And they're smart,

147
01:28:59.000 --> 01:29:35.000
so they could do that. But we got much smarter as a group. group when I shut up and I asked them for their opinion and they would go around the room and share all kinds of different perspectives, including ones that were quite different from mine and kind of caught blind spots that I had. And in those cases, we came up with much better solutions, more creative ideas, and we became more innovative and our research became better, became more impactful. And guess what? That's an environment that everybody loves. So it also became a place that people wanted to be part of that group more and more once they realized that there was kind of this vibe of intellectual humility. So it's something I'm trying to study now is how you can

148
01:29:35.000 --> 01:30:27.000
create this in other groups because I think this is really fundamental to, A, getting a shared sense of reality, but also being creative and innovative and creating a space that people want to be a part of. Now, being part of a group is something that most of the time I think we are able to choose, but sometimes this choice is made for us. That was the case with listener Radica. I'm wondering if we could explore group think when it comes to being put in a group that you don't necessarily want to be put into, right? So there's certain identities that you want to be part of who you are. But society puts you into buckets. Society puts labels on you that maybe you don't want to have on you.

149
01:30:27.000 --> 01:30:56.000
And maybe in the beginning, you sort of resist. or you fight those labels, and then eventually you're just maybe tired of fighting those labels, and you sort of just like sink into that identity. And you're like, well, I guess everyone is making me out to be this way. You know, maybe I am this way. Talk about the role of agency and choice in group membership, group participation, and group identity, Jay. The issue of agency in groups is crucially important.

