01:30:56.000 --> 01:31:24.000
The message that I try to send to people all the time is that you should be very, very thoughtful about the groups that you join because those groups will shape you. And that was something she mentioned that once you're part of the group and people will see you that way, you start to adapt to that view. And the other way in which the group shapes you is by their values and norms, you start to internalize those. So we should be very thoughtful about the groups we join because they're going to lead us to be our best selves or worse selves.

151
01:31:24.000 --> 01:32:00.000
And if you try to join the groups that are going to help you attain the type of identity that you want to have and achieve the goals you want, you're going to be a lot happier and more successful in life. Now, what she's talking about is the case that happens to all of us at some point is that you're going to get put into groups. And for many of us, those groups are our age, our gender, our ethnicity, where we're born, our family lineage, because we cannot control any of those. And people stereotype us on the basis of those things. And they often do it within 100 to 200 milliseconds of seeing somebody. Wow.

152
01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:24.000
This is what the neuroscience shows. You show somebody something within a few hundred milliseconds. They're often encoding their race, their gender, their age. And so we can't avoid that. And if people treat us differently on that basis, it's a challenge to escape it. And so this is why I strongly encourage us to, like, talk to people and be open and listen to who they are. And what identities they actually care about.

153
01:32:24.000 --> 01:33:08.000
I do this exercise in groups sometimes when I'm giving you. talks. I ask people to list the 10 most important identities to them. It's a great actual conversation starter at cocktail parties or work retreats because you learn a ton about a person by what identities actually matter to them. For me, that may be a father, an author, a professor, a New Yorker now, a hockey fan, but you start to learn about what I really value and the things that I don't actually care about that aren't central to me. But when you see me, you might not see those identities. They don't pop out from the picture of me. And so it is really important that we have an open mind and talk to people because they are all of these things. The classic phrase for it is

154
01:33:08.000 --> 01:33:50.000
we contain multitudes. And people don't like to be stereotyped and pigeonholed into an identity, especially one that they did not choose. And so I think we need to create a space for people to have more freedom and agency to select the identities and the groups that matter to them. And you'll see people do this. They'll signal it by wearing a hat of their favorite team or the city they're from or the country from the nation that they're born in. If that matters to them. And this is their way of sending a signal out into the world about the identities that matter to them and who they really are.

